put('44-03-03','Invariant relative ðe does not display case endings. The head ðā ðing is object of buy, while within the relative ðe supplies the complement required by beðurfan; its spelling does not transfer the head’s accusative to that internal role.',['U18G-FB52','U18G-BT95'],'u18-bethurfan-genitive-relative')
put('44-03-04','Beðurfan is a preterite-present: its present singular uses beðearf, plural beðurfon; weak dental past uses beðorfte/beðorfton. The complete twelve-cell indicative system is displayed, with the selected genitive frame separately taught.',['U18G-BT95','U18G-FB77'],'u18-bethurfan-indicative12')
put('44-04-03','Mā expresses greater quantity, more; it does not agree as a comparative adjective with brothers. Its complement is the genitive plural brōðra.',['U18G-FB45','U18G-FB90'],'actual prior mā plus partitive-genitive explanation')
put('44-04-04','Partitive genitive after a quantity: mā brōðra means more brothers. The brothers are the set whose quantity is compared.',['U18G-FB90'],'actual prior partitive genitive, explicitly restated')
put('44-05-05','Giernan takes genitive ðæs here; the following ðæt clause explains the thing desired. Retain both words: ðæs is the genitive demonstrative, ðæt the content conjunction.',['U18G-FB91','U18G-FB104'],'u18-giernan-propositional-desire')
put('44-05-06','The desired bringing is expressed by past subjunctive lǣdden under the former desire. The brothers are plural subject; the youngest brother is accusative object.',['U18G-FB104'],'u18-giernan-propositional-desire; u18-laedan-preterite-subjunctive6')
put('44-06-03','Sīe ic scyldig is an independent present-subjunctive wish or self-imprecation: let me be guilty. It is not a present indicative statement of guilt.',['U18G-FB103'],'actual prior optative; locally restated')
put('44-07-02','The source deliberately says were, pointing to intransitive wesan plus past participle cumen. In the complete supplied contrary condition, past subjunctive wǣren makes the coming anterior to the imagined result; do not replace it with unassigned habban plus an intransitive participle.',['U18G-FB102','U18G-FB105'],'U18-H-44-07')
put('44-07-04','Section176 is adverbial instrumental. A second time is ōðre sīðe: ōðre instrumental singular, sīðe the noun’s dative/instrumental singular. The sīð hint is a lemma, not a command to suppress the noun ending.',['U18G-FB99'],'U18-H-44-07; sith-u18-ins-sg')
put('44-08-01','Nīede is the supplied adverbial instrumental expressing necessity, necessarily/by necessity. With sculon it conveys must needs; do not treat it as a finite form of need.',['U18G-FB99'],'source local nīede help')
put('45-02-04','Āgiefe is third-singular present subjunctive in the purpose clause, so that he may restore the brother to you. Ēow is dative recipient and ēowerne brōðor accusative returned person.',['U18G-FB105'],'u18-agiefan-restoration')
put('45-02-06','The genitive denotes what someone has been deprived of: mīnra bearna remains genitive after passive eom berēafod. In the active frame the deprived person is accusative; the genitive possessions do not become the passive subject.',['U18G-FB91','U18G-BTS81'],'u18-bereafian-deprivation')
put('45-04-04','Tō middes dæges is the source-supplied time phrase at midday. Here tō governs a genitive; middes agrees with genitive dæges. Section166.1 supplies the parallel principle of adjective agreement within the prepositional phrase, not a direction to change this printed genitive to dative.',['U18G-FB92','U18G-FB95'],'source whole time-phrase support')
put('45-05-01','Cook177.10 has swā hiere beboden wæs. Our model is a declared lexical adaptation with bēodan: swā him boden wæs, as was commanded him. Him is a dative addressee in an impersonal passive, not a nominative subject.',['U18G-FB177','U18G-EX43','U18G-FB102'],'U18-H-45-05')
put('45-06-06','A past participle used predicatively may be uninflected. Thus plural wē can have base lǣded in wē sindon hider in lǣded. No new plural participial ending is required by the prompt.',['U18G-FB41'],'U18-H-45-06-PASSIVE')
put('45-07-03','Hlystan governs genitive speech/content, so ūrre sprǣce is genitive feminine singular. Hlyste is second-singular present subjunctive after the plea.',['U18G-FB91','U18G-FB104'],'u18-hlystan-genitive')
put('45-07-05','The original comment that “for ourselves” may be omitted permits either the visible benefactive/reflexive dative ūs or its omission. The model retains ūs after bohton: we bought wheat for ourselves. It is not an accusative thing purchased.',['U18G-FB93','U18G-FB100'],'actual prior dative benefit; explicit alternative')
put('45-09-03','On plus accusative marks putting something into a container. Ūre saccas is accusative plural after on in the clause about who put the money there; static location would instead take a dative.',['U18G-FB96','U18G-FB97'],'actual prior motion/location contrast')
put('46-01-01','Ondrǣdað ēow uses a reflexive dative: do not fear. The plural addressees are subject, and ēow refers back to them. It is not the object feared; no feared object is expressed in this item.',['U18G-FB92','U18G-FB93','U18G-FB100'],'u18-ondraedan-genitive')
put('46-02-01','Ðwēan is a contracted strong verb. Select the actual prior past plural ðwōgon for they washed; the headword’s contracted vowel does not supply the past stem by itself.',['U18G-FB58','U18G-FB65'],'actual prior ðwēan indicative table')
put('46-06-03','The relative pronoun ðe is invariant. It represents the boy as the person previously mentioned; mē is the dative person told. Omit English of as explicitly instructed instead of adding an unsupported preposition.',['U18G-FB52','U18G-BTS240'],'u18-foresecgean-past-mention')
put('46-07-01','The person commanded by bēodan is dative: his gerēfan. The direct imperative that follows gives the commanded act. This is the newly assigned command sense, distinct from the earlier offer sense.',['U18G-FB94','U18G-EX43'],'u18-beodan-command')
put('46-07-02','On plus accusative gives the destination into his own sack: on his āgenne sacc. The adjective and noun agree as accusative masculine singular.',['U18G-FB96','U18G-FB97'],'actual prior motion/location contrast')
put('46-09-06','The catching is prospective from the command: when you have caught them, then ask. Cook196b permits present subjunctive fō in that future temporal condition; the source’s you is singular here because Joseph addresses one steward. An English perfect does not force an OE perfect auxiliary.',['U18G-FB105'],'u18-prospective-when')
put('47-01-01','Forstelan belongs to strongIV: present e/i, past singular æ, past plural ǣ. Second-plural forstǣlon therefore differs from third-singular forstæl in47.8. The complete twelve-cell indicative system is displayed.',['U18G-FB63','U18G-FB55'],'u18-forstelan-indicative12')
put('47-03-03','Finde is second-singular present subjunctive in the relative under the optative/hypothetical sīe hē ofslægen. Section195 describes this subjunctive attraction; it does not require subjunctive in every relative clause.',['U18G-FB104'],'u18-relative-subjunctive-attraction')
put('48-02-03','For plus accusative marks substitution: for hine means instead of him/in his place. It is distinct from the causal for + dative used of the money in45.6.',['U18G-FB96','U18G-FB97'],'source supplied substitution phrase')
put('48-04-02','Diernan is a long weakI verb; the assigned system here is the complete plain/inflected infinitive pair diernan/diernanne, not a silently expanded finite paradigm. The plain infinitive follows past ability meahte, and accusative hine is reflexive.',['U18G-FB68','U18G-FB106'],'u18-diernan-infinitive2; u18-diernan-reflexive')
put('48-05-03','Hlūdre stefne is a dative/instrumental manner phrase, with a loud voice; no extra mid is required. The feminine adjective agrees with singular stefne.',['U18G-FB93'],'U18-H-48-05')
put('48-06-01','Reflexive dative ēow belongs with fear; nānes ðinges is the separately licensed genitive feared object. The negation is concordant: do not fear anything, not an affirmative double-negative reading.',['U18G-FB92','U18G-FB93','U18G-FB100'],'u18-ondraedan-genitive')
put('48-06-03','Ofðynce is third-singular impersonal subjunctive, let it not grieve/displease you. Dative ēow gives the experiencers; the following ðæt clause gives what troubles them. Plural experiencers do not cause plural verbal agreement.',['U18G-FB102'],'u18-ofthyncean-impersonal')
put('48-07-02','Ofer with accusative ealle eorðan marks extent over all the earth. The noun’s identical weak ending does not make it a dative; the full phrase has the accusative relation required by172.',['U18G-FB96','U18G-FB97'],'U18-H-48-07')
put('48-07-07','Nāhwæðer means neither. In the supplied whole clause, nāhwæðer with paired ne ... ne excludes plowing and reaping under singular mæg; mann is the singular indefinite subject. The lexical hint89a alone does not teach the coordination, so the complete negative frame is visible.',['U18G-FB52','U18G-BTS646'],'U18-H-48-07')
put('48-07-08','The source spells ęrian, with ogonek, and assigns short weakI116. The complete assigned nonfinite pair is ęrian/ęrianne; only plain ęrian occurs after mæg. No new full finite weakI system is inferred.',['U18G-FB71','U18G-FB106'],'u18-erian-infinitive2')
put('48-07-09','Rīpan is strongI102. The complete assigned infinitive pair rīpan/rīpanne preserves the source’s long ī; only plain rīpan occurs after mæg. A class reference does not itself assign all finite forms.',['U18G-FB59','U18G-FB106'],'u18-ripan-infinitive2')
put('48-08-05','Him is the dative addressee of plural command bēodað; the father is subject of cume and wunie in the commanded ðæt clause. Keep the commanding brothers distinct from the father who is to come and dwell.',['U18G-FB94','U18G-FB104'],'u18-beodan-command')
nominals={'44-08-05':'hunig','44-08-06':'æcern','45-03-02':'feoh','45-04-02':'gerēfa','45-06-04':'sacc','45-07-04':'hwǣte','46-08-01':'læfel','46-08-02':'weorð','47-11-04':'scēawere'}
